    pr_ref:  # 00c2
    description: "## Problem\n\n24 protocol/framework linkers under `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/` apply regex `.finditer()` directly to raw file bytes (`content = file_path.read_text(...)` then `for match in PATTERN.finditer(content)`). Their pattern strings — describing code shapes like `producer.send('topic', msg)` — match against the linker's own module docstring, which documents those exact patterns in prose. So when hypergumbo analyzes itself (or any repo whose docs include code-shape examples), the linker emits phantom nodes from comments and docstrings.\n\n## Evidence (hypergumbo self-analysis 3.0.0, tests excluded)\n\n| kind | non-test count | in linker source | FP rate |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| websocket_endpoint | 7 | 6 | 86% |\n| mq_subscriber | 26 | 26 | 100% |\n| mq_publisher | 19 | 18 | 95% |\n| event_publisher | 39 | 31 | 79% |\n| event_subscriber | 27 | 12 | 44% |\n| db_query | 13 | 4 | 31% |\n| subprocess_call | 5 | 4 | 80% |\n| http_client | 4 | 3 | 75% |\n| graphql_resolver | 8 | 8 | 100% (hypergumbo has no GraphQL) |\n\nSample FP node from `linkers/message_queue.py:12` (a docstring line):\n```\nname='kafka:subscribe:topic'\nspan={start_line: 12, end_line: 12}\norigin=message-queue-linker-v1\nmeta={message_type: subscribe, queue_type: kafka, topic: topic, topic_type: literal}\n```\n\nLine 12 of that file is `- consumer.subscribe(['topic']) -> message_subscribe` inside the module docstring.\n\n## Affected linker modules (24)\n\n`crypto_flow`, `database_query`, `di_resolution`, `event_sourcing`, `graphql`, `graphql_resolver`, `grpc`, `http`, `lua_ffi`, `message_dispatch`, `message_queue`, `napi`, `openapi`, `orm`, `pyffi`, `react_component`, `ruby_ffi`, `subprocess_cli`, `swift_objc`, `tauri_ipc`, `wasm_bindgen`, `websocket`, `yjs_crdt`, `annotation_convention`.\n\nIdentified by: `grep -rln \"_scan_file\\|read_text.*finditer\\|content = file_path.read_text\" packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/linkers/`.\n\nSeveral of their docstrings explicitly defend the choice (\"Regex-based detection is fast and doesn't require tree-sitter\" — `websocket.py:59`, `ipc.py:63`, `phoenix_ipc.py:29`, `go_cobra.py:38`).\n\n## Invariant\n\nPattern detectors that match code shapes must not match against the contents of comments or language-level docstrings.\n\n## Fix shape\n\nAdd a shared masker `mask_doc_regions(content: str, language: str) -> str` (new file `linkers/_text_filters.py`) that:\n\n1. Parses with tree-sitter for the given language.\n2. Walks the tree collecting byte ranges of:\n   - **All languages:** `comment`, `line_comment`, `block_comment`, `multiline_comment` nodes (reuses the set already defined in `analyze/base.py:565`).\n   - **Python only:** any `string` whose immediate parent is `expression_statement` AND whose grandparent is `module`, `function_definition.body`, or `class_definition.body` (docstring discriminator by position).\n3. Replaces those byte ranges with spaces, preserving newlines so existing line counters in linkers stay correct.\n4. Returns the masked content.\n\nPer-linker change is ~2 lines added after `read_text`:\n```python\ncontent = file_path.read_text(...)\ncontent = mask_doc_regions(content, language)  # NEW\nfor match in PATTERN.finditer(content):\n    ...\n```\n\n## Why mask docstrings but not regular string literals\n\nSeveral linkers (`database_query`, `graphql`, `openapi`) match the inside of regular string literals — that's their whole job. Masking all strings would regress those. Comments are always-safe to mask (no linker should care about comment contents). Python module/function/class docstrings are special-cased because they're the dominant FP carrier and never carry executable patterns.\n\nAdd a kwarg `mask_string_literals_too=False` for forward-compat with Elixir's `@doc\"\"\"...\"\"\"` (deferred — not in default 24-linker scan paths).\n\n## Cross-language design (Q1 resolved)\n\nPython is the only mainstream language with prose-as-string. Java/Kotlin/Scala/Swift/C#/C/C++/PHP/Rust/Go/Ruby/Lua/Haskell/R/JS/TS/TSX all store doc-prose in comment-family nodes — covered by the universal rule. Elixir `@doc\"\"\"...\"\"\"` is deferred (not in current scan paths).\n\n## Missing-grammar handling (Q2 resolved)\n\n- Grammar unavailable for language → return content unchanged (fail-closed; no regression vs. today). Emit one DEBUG log per (file, language).\n- Parse error producing a partial tree → walk it as normal, mask whatever comment ranges were found. Don't mask `ERROR` nodes.\n- Unknown language string → same as missing grammar.\n\nThe contract: the masker never reduces detections; it only ever removes FPs. All failure modes bias to fail-closed.\n\n## Application strategy (Q4a resolved)\n\nApply uniformly, immediately after `read_text`, in every one of the 24 linkers. No linker in the set legitimately wants to match comment or Python-docstring contents. Future linkers should use a shared `_read_source(file_path, language)` helper that bakes in the mask.\n\n## Expected FP reduction (hypergumbo self-analysis)\n\nBased on the table above, ~112 / 158 non-test FPs eliminated. Real detections in code (e.g., the 9 legitimate `db_query` nodes in `tracker/cache.py`) survive because they're not in docstrings/comments.\n\n## Quantitative bakeoff claim — `awaits_bakeoff_validation`\n\nThis PR carries a quantitative claim (\"~112 FP reduction on hypergumbo self-analysis; aggregate FP-rate movement on the bakeoff corpus expected\"). The merging PR must receive the `awaits_bakeoff_validation` tag at merge time per the bakeoff validation discipline in AGENTS.md. Discharge requires a DEEP cycle that verifies the FP-rate movement on a cohort that contains repos with prose-heavy docstrings/comments documenting code shapes.\n\n## Test plan\n\nMasker tests (independent of linkers, ~10 cases):\n- Python docstring containing `producer.send('topic')` → masked\n- Python regular string containing same → unmasked\n- Python comment → masked\n- JS line comment with pattern → masked\n- JS template literal with pattern → unmasked\n- Java javadoc (block_comment) with pattern → masked\n- File with syntax error → partial mask, no crash\n- Grammar missing → content returned unchanged\n- Empty file → no crash\n- Newlines preserved (line numbers stay correct after masking)\n\nPer-linker regression tests (one assertion each, 24 linkers): assert that a fixture file containing a pattern in its module docstring produces 0 nodes for that pattern's kind.\n\n100% coverage required (existing repo policy).\n\n## Out of scope (filed as follow-ups)\n\n- Cross-linker tree-sitter parse cache via `LinkerContext.parsed_trees` (performance; separate `todo_soft`).\n- Elixir `@doc\"\"\"...\"\"\"` (string-with-docstring-semantics; deferred until a linker scans Elixir).\n\n## Source\n\nSelf-analysis dogfooding session, hypergumbo 3.0.0 @ d5e3941c6, 2026-04-29."  # 00c2
